      "browser": "fail",
      "why": "this example shows a run-time error",
      "expect_error": "call depth quota exceeded"
     }
    }
   },
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-functions-md-9",
   "kind": {
    "type": "markdown"
   },
   "source": "## Higher-order functions and lambdas\n\n> **Not yet implemented.** Passing a function by name (`apply(double, 21)`),\n> storing one in a variable (`let f = double`), and anonymous functions\n> (`|x| { ... }`, `|x| => x * 2`) all fail to compile. The lexer reads a bare `|`\n> as an identifier, so the lambda parser is unreachable (crush-ast **CRUSH-75**,\n> open); closures that capture variables are a separate, larger gap. Until then,\n> write ordinary named functions and call them directly, or use the\n> pipeline operator (`x |> f`), which does accept a function **name**:\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "done"
   },
   "meta": {},
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-functions-5",
   "kind": {
    "type": "crush"
   },
   "source": "fn double(n) {\n    return n * 2\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let f = double\n    print(f(21))\n}\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "pending"
   },
